{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The non-expert panel will not, on average, be more reliable because the checklist is strictly dominated by the expert's mental model: any rule expressible as a lossy linear projection of optimal features is already implicitly contained within the expert's richer intuition, which can apply the same weighting without discarding orthogonal, outcome-relevant context the checklist ignores.","counterconditions":["Checklist compliance exhibits accuracy gains when orthogonal expert cues are systematically biased or noise-corrupted in the expert's intuition.","Checklist items are not linearly separable from outcome-relevant features, but instead encode non-linear interactions that an expert's intuition systematically fails to capture.","Expert intuition varies prohibitively across individuals, making the 'average' expert less reliable than a calibrated panel even if the panel's information is a strict subset.","The checklist is dynamically updated by the expert during evaluation, transforming it from a lossy projection into a complete state-transition operator."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists function as lossy linear projections of the true outcome-relevant feature space. A trained expert's intuition implements a superset of any such projection, retaining orthogonal context. On tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the expert's retention of orthogonal cues strictly bounds the panel's achievable accuracy from above.","optional_refs":["90f5d47db03c9cfdee5e2be5da780731e031f8672f314af4b5bdf17d5f2c207f","27529970a74774c8d3eba40bd7a324f6425a9442dcc664579f0895b2627ac246","e2e7d6b93ae72267573841699576338b786e6e42ab3bdd5746c3c499af9c2509"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel will be more reliable on average, but only because the checklist externalizes the stopping rule and prevents the premature closure that dominates solo expert intuition. Genuine reliability is distinguished from mere compliance by testing if perturbing decision-relevant features (while preserving the checklist's formal structure) degrades performance—mere compliance tracks the right answer only when the perturbation is orthogonal to the checklist's explicit logic.","counterconditions":["Experts demonstrate no premature closure bias in the specific task domain.","Perturbing decision-relevant features changes the expert's judgment but not the panel's, indicating the expert is using richer cues while the panel is blindly following the lossy checklist.","The checklist's formal structure happens to perfectly correlate with the outcome-relevant features in the evaluation set, but fails on out-of-distribution cases (mere compliance).","Expert intuition is explicitly calibrated against premature closure, neutralizing the checklist's advantage."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists force complete enumeration of the evidence space, mitigating bounded rationality. Solo experts rely on recognition-primed decision making, which is vulnerable to stopping at the first plausible hypothesis. The reliability gain is real if perturbing actual outcome-relevant features changes the panel's judgment, proving they are evaluating substance, not just matching formal protocol criteria.","optional_refs":["758b4f8985f9b63813b2692b3c13638da82f8db7793f1dd44514ac0a90a06175","e65f3f0250ab390a14d77d9c8bba21e0f11c406a5b8a45b4154486d0a8b15ae3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The claim is categorically false for any objectively verifiable task: a linear projection (checklist) can never systematically outperform the full-rank operator (expert intuition) because the expert can always choose to apply the checklist mechanically if needed, whereas the panel cannot access the orthogonal information the expert retains. What appears as a panel reliability gain is always an artifact of evaluating on a narrow feature distribution.","counterconditions":["The expert's intuition is so noisy that it functionally acts as a lower-rank operator than the checklist due to internal cognitive biases.","The cost of the expert accessing the 'checklist mode' is prohibitively high, making the bounded rationality limitation a practical, not just theoretical, barrier.","The panel's diversity (Condorcet effect) introduces orthogonal information not possessed by the single expert, making the panel the higher-rank operator.","The objective outcome is governed entirely by the linear projection, rendering the expert's orthogonal information pure noise."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The expert's cognitive state is a superset of the panel's cognitive state. Any linear projection can be trivially recovered from a higher-dimensional space, but not vice versa. Thus, the expert strictly dominates the panel in theoretical capacity. Observed panel superiority must be an artifact of restricting the evaluation to cases where the orthogonal dimensions are constant or irrelevant.","optional_refs":["1c69549e200df0651f2dd728f72da68dc5441f6d66965f1e8300e4c97731e03e","90f5d47db03c9cfdee5e2be5da780731e031f8672f314af4b5bdf17d5f2c207f","7c74d25c5f739e92a081b0b876a1fa978794df38673a32b16859f65f1ef9b9a6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if the checklist acts as a state-transition operator whose full execution path is required to reach the correct outcome. Mere checklist compliance that tracks the right answer is distinguished by varying the sequence of checklist items: if shuffling the order degrades performance, the items are functionally dependent and the gain is genuine; if performance is invariant to order, the checklist is merely a set of correlated proxies (compliance) rather than a causal mechanism.","counterconditions":["The task outcome is determined by a simple additive model where order genuinely does not matter, making order-invariance a sign of correct modeling rather than spurious compliance.","The expert's intuition is also a state-transition operator, but executes the transitions faster, neutralizing the panel's advantage.","Checklist order is strictly enforced by external protocol, making the shuffle test impossible to administer.","The expert intuition relies on a different set of correlated proxies that happen to track the outcome better in the evaluation set."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklist compliance tracks right answers via spurious correlation when items are independent proxies. Genuine reliability arises when checklist items form a dependency graph (state-transition) where step N requires the state generated by step N-1. Ordering sensitivity is the within-evaluation evidence of genuine mechanistic reliance versus proxy correlation.","optional_refs":["27529970a74774c8d3eba40bd7a324f6425a9442dcc664579f0895b2627ac246","e2e7d6b93ae72267573841699576338b786e6e42ab3bdd5746c3c499af9c2509","7c74d25c5f739e92a081b0b876a1fa978794df38673a32b16859f65f1ef9b9a6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel will not outperform the expert because the Condorcet jury theorem does not apply: individual panelist errors in applying the checklist are perfectly correlated (they all received the same flawed training and apply the same lossy rules), meaning aggregation does not reduce noise. The expert, despite intuition's variance, at least retains the possibility of idiosyncratic correct insight. Genuine reliability would require demonstrated panelist independence, which a shared checklist explicitly destroys.","counterconditions":["Panelists apply the checklist with highly variable interpretations, accidentally introducing independence.","The expert's intuition is systematically biased in the same direction as the checklist's bias, making the expert just as correlated.","The task is so complex that even with correlated checklist application, the reduction in cognitive load provides a net benefit over the expert's bounded rationality.","The checklist is probabilistically, not deterministically, applied, preserving some independence."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The Condorcet jury theorem requires independent voter errors. A shared checklist induces perfectly correlated errors across non-experts. Thus, the panel acts as a single 'checklist agent' with n=1 effective size, removing the aggregation benefit. The expert, as a separate cognitive system, provides the only true independence. Within-evaluation evidence of mere compliance is a perfect inter-rater agreement that matches the checklist's biases.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","1c69549e200df0651f2dd728f72da68dc5441f6d66965f1e8300e4c97731e03e","e65f3f0250ab390a14d77d9c8bba21e0f11c406a5b8a45b4154486d0a8b15ae3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.15},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The question presents a false dichotomy: the most reliable judgment system is neither the non-expert panel nor the single expert, but a coupled system where the expert dynamically generates the checklist and the non-expert panel executes it. The panel's reliability gain over a solo expert is mere compliance if the checklist is static, but becomes a genuine gain when the checklist fully specifies the optimal state-transition operator, which requires the expert's continuous input.","counterconditions":["The solo expert can execute the dynamic checklist internally without cognitive cost, rendering the panel unnecessary.","The expert's rule generation is itself biased, making the panel's execution of a flawed dynamic checklist worse than the expert's holistic intuition.","The task is simple enough that the overhead of expert-rule-generation plus panel-execution provides no benefit over direct expert execution.","The panel fails to execute the dynamically generated checklist accurately."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Static checklists are lossy projections (SRC_004, SRC_005). Dynamic checklists generated by an expert can become full state-transition operators (SRC_007). A panel executing a dynamic checklist outperforms a solo expert because it separates the generation of the decision rule from its execution, mitigating expert cognitive bias in execution while preserving expert knowledge in rule generation.","optional_refs":["27529970a74774c8d3eba40bd7a324f6425a9442dcc664579f0895b2627ac246","e2e7d6b93ae72267573841699576338b786e6e42ab3bdd5746c3c499af9c2509","7c74d25c5f739e92a081b0b876a1fa978794df38673a32b16859f65f1ef9b9a6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.05}],"simulation_proposals":[]}